<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Classroom News – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Classroom News</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Will Write the Article About Our School Trip.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Compose simple descriptive texts. interactive: Write specific comments to peers to improve a writing product in the form of feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose descriptive texts about recent class events (e.g., 'Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the museum.'). Can write constructive feedback for classmates' newsletter articles, including compliments and suggestions (e.g., 'Your story about the school trip is fun to read. You can add more details about the activities.'). Can write suggestions for content (e.g., 'We need more pictures for the classroom newsletter.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a recent school trip (e.g., "Our trip to the Emberá Community"). Students brainstorm words related to the picture and the trip. The teacher writes students' ideas on the board (e.g., 'fun', 'river', 'boat', 'people', 'dance', 'food', 'exciting'). The teacher asks, "What do we want to write about this exciting event for our classroom newsletter?" (Grammar: Present simple question, Vocabulary: exciting, event, classroom, newsletter, write).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to plan a simple article by asking questions and writing down answers: "What will I write about? The school trip. What happened? Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the Emberá Community. What did we see? Traditional houses and art. What else do we need? Pictures for the newsletter." The teacher emphasizes the 'go' in "I'll go" if used (Pronunciation: Emphasize the main verb in future tense contractions). The teacher then writes a simple headline idea like "Our Fun Trip!" and shows a basic paragraph structure.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we going to read a story or write a story today?" (Expected answer: Write a story.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write about the future trip or the past trip?" (Expected answer: The past trip.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple, short example newsletter article about a school event (e.g., "Yesterday, our class went to the zoo. We saw monkeys and lions. It was fun! We need more pictures for our newsletter.") written on a large poster or displayed on the projector. The article highlights key vocabulary (article, newsletter, pictures, event, fun) and grammatical structures (Past Simple: went, saw, was; Present Simple Q: need; Future Simple: We will take more photos). Short vowel /ɑ/ in 'class' and long vowel /oʊ/ in 'show' (if applicable in the example) are briefly pointed out (Phonemic awareness).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Narrating past events: 'Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the Emberá Community.' Request materials: 'We need pictures for the newsletter.' Sociolinguistic: Collaborate in writing projects by clarifying roles and using appropriate language for teamwork.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the example article silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this article about?" (Expected: A school trip/zoo trip.) "What do they need for the newsletter?" (Expected: Pictures.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The article is about a trip next week." (False) "They need photos for the article." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing simple descriptive texts about past events using Past Simple, stating future plans using Future Simple, and making requests using Present Simple questions. Target vocabulary: article, newsletter, event, pictures, describe, write, edit, add, fun, exciting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames on a worksheet related to the school trip and newsletter:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Yesterday, our class _______ (go) to the Emberá Community.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">We _______ (see) traditional houses and _______ (learn) about their culture.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">It _______ (be) a very _______ (exciting/fun) event.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I _______ (will/write) the _______ (article) about this trip.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What _______ (else/we/need) for our _______ (classroom/newsletter)?</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students complete the sentences with the correct verb forms (past simple, future simple) and vocabulary. (Grammar: Past simple, Future simple, Present simple questions. Vocabulary: article, class, event, exciting, fun, newsletter, write, see, learn).
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Paragraph Expansion (Semi-controlled):</b> In pairs, students receive a short, basic sentence (e.g., "Our trip was great."). They must add two more sentences to describe the trip in detail, using words from the board (from the warm-up) and target vocabulary. For example, "Our trip was great. We saw a lot of interesting art. We learned about traditional dances." (Vocabulary: art, interesting, describe. Pragmatic: Narrating past events).
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Sentence Check:</b> Pairs swap their expanded sentences with another pair. They read their classmates' sentences and check for correct grammar (Past Simple verbs) and vocabulary use. They write a compliment and one suggestion for improvement, such as "Your sentences are clear. You can add one more detail about the food." (Sociolinguistic: Collaborate in writing projects, using appropriate language for teamwork: compliments and suggestions. Writing: Write specific comments to peers).
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Writing and Peer Feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Draft an Article:</b> Students will individually compose a simple descriptive text (a short article) for the "Our Classroom News" newsletter about "Our School Trip to the Emberá Community." They must include:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A headline (e.g., "An Exciting Emberá Trip!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least three sentences describing what happened on the trip (Past Simple: 'Yesterday, we went...', 'We saw...', 'It was fun!').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One sentence stating their role or a need for the newsletter (Future Simple: 'I will describe the activities.', Present Simple Q: 'What else do we need for this article?').</li>
                        </ul>
                        (Writing: Compose simple descriptive texts. Grammar: Past simple, Future simple, Present simple with questions. Vocabulary: headline, article, describe, event, fun, exciting, pictures, newsletter).
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Feedback:</b> Students exchange their written articles with a classmate. Each student reads their partner's article and writes specific, constructive feedback directly on their partner's paper. They must include:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One compliment (e.g., 'Your headline is creative.').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One suggestion for improvement (e.g., 'You can add more details about the food.').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A suggestion for content (e.g., 'We need more pictures for this article.').</li>
                        </ul>
                        (Writing: Write specific comments to peers to improve a writing product in the form of feedback. Learning Outcome: Can write constructive feedback for classmates' newsletter articles. Sociolinguistic: Collaborate in writing projects, using appropriate language for teamwork.)
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment forms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their draft article along with the peer feedback they received, and the feedback they wrote for their partner's article.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses Past Simple to describe events.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses Future Simple to describe plans (e.g., 'I will write').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., article, newsletter, pictures, event).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Composes a simple descriptive text (3+ sentences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provides constructive peer feedback (compliment + suggestion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes suggestions for content (e.g., 'We need more pictures.').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word you used in your article today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write feedback for your classmate?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the article was the most fun for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you want to improve in your writing next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully composed simple descriptive texts about the school trip, integrating Past Simple verbs. The peer feedback activity was engaging, with students using phrases like 'Your story is fun to read' and 'You can add more details'. Some students needed scaffolding for the Future Simple sentences, requiring more examples of 'I will write' or 'We will organize'. Next time, I will provide sentence starters for the future plans part of the article. The integration of vocabulary like 'article', 'newsletter', 'event', and 'pictures' was evident in most student work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Mediation & Integration), students will revise their articles based on the feedback received and contribute to creating the final classroom newsletter layout. This will involve summarizing information and working collaboratively to publish their work, reinforcing the 21st-century skills project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>